{"records":[{"id":"z5s9xnkx6n","title":"Original WB Li and Kang et. al. Developmental Cell","description":"The neurotransmitter GABA has been thought to be involved in the development of some types of cancer. Yet, the de novo synthesis of GABA and how it functions in hepatocellular carcinoma (HCC) remain unclear. Here, we report that SLC6A12 acts as a transporter of GABA and ALDH9A1 (aldehyde dehydrogenase 9 family member A1), not GAD1 (glutamate decarboxylase 1), generates GABA in human HCC. Interestingly, SLC6A12 and ALDH9A1 are upregulated during lung metastases of HCC, and depletion of either of them leads to impaired HCC metastasis. Mechanistically, GABA directly binds and stabilizes β-catenin, resulting in activated Wnt/β-catenin signaling, and thereby enhanced HCC metastasis. Reciprocally, β-catenin transcriptionally upregulates SLC6A12 to import more GABA to stabilize β-catenin. Thus, our findings identify ALDH9A1 is the major GABA synthetase in HCC, demonstrate a positive-feedback regulatory mechanism for sustaining Wnt/β-catenin signaling, and find a role for β-catenin in sensing GABA that contributes to HCC metastasis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-11-26T18:16:58.604Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z5s9xnkx6n","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z5s9xnkx6n"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Li","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"477bs297n5","title":"A Study of PKCα-mediated nuclear translocation of cGAS stabilizes β-catenin and drives metastasis. Qimin Zhang et al","description":"Although recent studies have shown that 2′3′-cyclic GMP-AMP synthase (cGAS) has both canonical cytoplasmic and noncanonical nuclear functions, the role of nuclear cGAS in cancer metastasis remains unclear. Here, we identify a STING-independent mechanism by which nuclear cGAS activates Wnt/β-catenin signaling to promote metastatic dissemination in triple-negative breast cancer (TNBC). Cytosolic protein kinase C alpha (PKCα) phosphorylates cGAS at Ser120, facilitating its nuclear translocation. Once in the nucleus, cGAS disrupts the interaction between β-catenin and tripartite motif-containing protein 33 (TRIM33), an E3 ubiquitin ligase, thereby preventing β-catenin ubiquitination and promoting its stabilization. This, in turn, activates Wnt/β-catenin signaling. PKCα thus acts as a key regulator of this non-canonical, cGAS-driven metastatic pathway. Therapeutic TAT peptides designed to inhibit cGAS phosphorylation significantly reduce metastatic dissemination. Clinically, elevated nuclear cGAS expression is associated with increased metastasis in TNBC cohorts. Together, these findings delineate a PKCα–cGAS–TRIM33 axis that regulates nuclear β-catenin stability and establish cGAS phosphorylation as a promising theragnostic target for TNBC metastasis.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-22T09:35:51.176Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/477bs297n5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/477bs297n5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Qimin","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","grant_id":"82172823, 32450566, and 81901621"},{"id":"https://ror.org/04323m874","name":"Science and Technology Department of Sichuan Province","grant_id":"2024NSFJQ0047"},{"id":"https://ror.org/0426zh255","name":"China Postdoctoral Science Foundation","grant_id":"2019M652624"}],"categories":["Metastasis","Ubiquitylation","Triple-Negative Breast Cancer","cGAS-STING Signaling Pathway"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xvk2gk4hw3","title":"Data of Murine Trophoblast Organoids as a Model for Trophoblast Development and CRISPR/Cas9 Screening","description":"Raw data for figures in Murine Trophoblast Organoids as a Model for Trophoblast Development and CRISPR/Cas9 Screening","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-25T15:27:04.321Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xvk2gk4hw3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xvk2gk4hw3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Qian","last_name":"Mao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Developmental Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"sxrvzkr4hh","title":"Pooled in vitro and in vivo CRISPR-Cas9 screening identifies tumor suppressors in human colon organoids","description":"UMI validation screen, original data from in vivo tumor suppressor CRISPR/Cas9 screening experiment. Figure 6.\nExperimental setup: 281 tumor suppressor gRNAs, positive controls, neutral controls and gRNAs targeting essential genes were introduced into the CRISPR-UMI lentivirus (Michlits et al., 2017). Transduced AK organoids (APC-KO and KRASG12D) were transplanted subcutaneously in NSG mice followed by barcode sequencing of the tumor pool (from 10 mice) and 5 individual tumors. Clone and read number were determined by next generation sequencing. The conventional gRNA enrichment analysis was compared to UMI-CRISPR-screening with outlier removal.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-04-20T15:46:17.868Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sxrvzkr4hh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sxrvzkr4hh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Henner","last_name":"Farin","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dfm3zy7h8z","title":"Targeting KRT17 inhibits ovarian cancer progression and platinum resistance by suppressing cancer stemness through the EPN1/Wnt/β -catenin axis----PART1","description":"Data for paper “KRT17 Promotes Ovarian Cancer Stemness，Progression, and Resistance to Platinum via Stabilization of EPN1 and Activation of the Wnt/β-catenin Pathway”---PARTI","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-05T13:18:29.517Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dfm3zy7h8z","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dfm3zy7h8z"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"G","last_name":"du","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cszpw4c66t","title":"Structural insights into Cas9-mediated prespacer selection in CRISPR-Cas adaptation. Gaizauskaite et al.","description":"During CRISPR-Cas adaptation, prokaryotic cells become immunized by the insertion of foreign DNA fragments, termed spacers, into the host genome to serve as templates for RNA-guided immunity. Spacer acquisition relies on the Cas1-Cas2 integrase and accessory proteins, which select DNA sequences flanked by the protospacer adjacent motif (PAM) and insert them into the CRISPR array. It has been shown that in type II-A systems selection of PAM-proximal prespacers is mediated by the effector nuclease Cas9, which forms a ‘supercomplex’ with the Cas1-Cas2 integrase and the Csn2 protein. Here, we present cryo-electron microscopy structures of the type II-A prespacer selection supercomplex in the DNA-scanning and two distinct PAM-bound configurations, providing insights into the mechanism of Cas9-mediated prespacer selection in type II-A CRISPR-Cas systems. Repurposing of Cas9 by the CRISPR adaptation machinery for prespacer selection characterized here demonstrates Cas9 plasticity and expands our knowledge of the Cas9 biology.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-02-20T09:45:34.481Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cszpw4c66t","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cszpw4c66t"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Ugne","last_name":"Gaizauskaite","orcid":"0009-0008-9137-5428"},{"first_name":"Giedre","last_name":"Tamulaitiene","orcid":"0000-0002-9794-3544"},{"first_name":"Arunas","last_name":"Silanskas","orcid":"0000-0002-6385-6928"},{"first_name":"Giedrius","last_name":"Gasiunas","orcid":"0000-0003-2509-9054"},{"first_name":"Virginijus","last_name":"Siksnys","orcid":"0000-0003-1983-2096"},{"first_name":"Giedrius","last_name":"Sasnauskas","orcid":"0000-0002-3185-2682"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/02gs16m83","name":"Lietuvos Mokslo Taryba","grant_id":"S-MIP-19-32"}],"categories":["Biochemistry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3cy28w7v9k","title":"RNA-seq profiling of 634T murine KP NSCLC cells deleted for AMPK subjected to no glucose conditions","description":"This dataset was generated for the paper, \"Genetic analysis reveals AMPK is required to support tumor growth in murine Kras-dependent lung cancer models.\"  Murine Kras mutant, p53 null (KP) 634T NSCLC cells were subjected to the CRISPR/Cas9 system including either a control gRNA (“WT”), or a gRNA targeting both AMPKalpha1 and AMPKalpha2 resulting in AMPK knockout in a polyclonal pooled cell population (“KO”). \"R1\" indicates Replicate 1, \"R2\" indicates Replicate 2. These cells were subjected to no glucose (0mM, \"NG\") conditions for 16 hours, and profiled by RNA-sequencing. \n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-10T16:47:21.29Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3cy28w7v9k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3cy28w7v9k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Lillian","last_name":"Eichner","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Reuben","last_name":"Shaw","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Lung Cancer","RNA Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bsdc4jmhwm","title":"Endothelial Shp2 deficiency controls alternative activation of macrophage preventing radiation-induced lung injury through Notch signaling","description":"Our findings demonstrate that endothelial Shp2 is a key regulator in radiation-induced lung injury by maintaining the radiation-induced Jag1 level through the β-catenin pathway, thereby reducing alternative macrophage activation to relieve radiation-induced lung injury. Two short-term and a long-term mouse model were used to evaluate the role of endothelial Shp2 in radiation-induced lung injury. The in vitro co-culture system was used to investigate the relationship between irradiated-endothelium and macrophage. HUVECs and MLECs were used here for in vitro study. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-01-18T21:17:50.223Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bsdc4jmhwm","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bsdc4jmhwm"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xue","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Communication","Mouse Model"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"t98pjcd7t6","title":"Single-cell lineage tracing of metastatic cancer reveals selection of hybrid EMT states","description":"The underpinnings of cancer metastasis remain poorly understood, in part due to a lack of tools for probing their emergence at high resolution. Here we present macsGESTALT, an inducible CRISPR-Cas9-based lineage recorder with highly efficient single-cell capture of both transcriptional and phylogenetic information. Applying macsGESTALT to a mouse model of metastatic pancreatic cancer, we recover ~380,000 CRISPR target sites and reconstruct dissemination of ~28,000 single cells across multiple metastatic sites. We find cells occupy a continuum of epithelial-to-mesenchymal transition (EMT) states. Metastatic potential peaks in rare, late-hybrid EMT states, which are aggressively selected from a predominately epithelial ancestral pool. The gene signatures of these late-hybrid EMT states are predictive of reduced survival in both human pancreatic and lung cancer patients, highlighting their relevance to clinical disease progression. Finally, we observe evidence for in vivo propagation of S100 family gene expression across clonally distinct metastatic subpopulations.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-06-29T09:16:44.113Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/t98pjcd7t6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/t98pjcd7t6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kamen","last_name":"Simeonov","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Pancreatic Cancer","Metastasis","S100A Gene","Cell Lineage","Epithelial Mesenchymal Transition","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":132667,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":153,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":132667,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":132097,"restricted":65,"embargoed":505},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2216},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1388},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":619},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":460},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":441},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":419},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico","count":414},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":368},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":319},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":269}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":130049},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1901},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":185},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":132},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":75},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":64},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":55},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":41},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":30},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":899},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":822},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":501},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":404},{"id":"f58d7a83-6efa-5d8a-a6c2-b9e78ee14783","name":"Maharishi Markandeshwar Institute of Physiotherapy and Rehabilitation","count":362}]}}